        { "host": "A", "text": "Five organizations: the Combat Capabilities Development Command, the Medical Research and Development Command, the 75th Innovation Command, Joint Modernization Command and the Transformation Decision Analysis Center. All five corps will test equipment and tactics for autonomous missions, and LaNeve said Fort Rucker will serve as the Army's drone autonomy centre of excellence." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "So this is bigger than a drone programme." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "LaNeve said the effort goes beyond drones. Folding medical research and the innovation command into an autonomy command is a structural choice, not a procurement. The Army hasn't named who will lead it, and our two sources disagree on LaNeve's own title and on how many subordinate commands there are." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Military, defense & geopolitics",
      "headline": "Army launches Project Gatekeeper on October 23, an AI-assisted sandbox to vet commercial software in minutes",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "And there's an AI system going on the approval path for software entering Army networks." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Breaking Defense reports Project Gatekeeper launches on October 23rd, 2026. It's a cybersecurity sandbox on the Army's cloud, walled off from operational networks and sensitive data. Software gets uploaded, run through the Army's standard security tools including an AI assessment, and within minutes it either passes or comes back with detail on what failed." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Who can use it?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Companies can submit without an Army contract, a program manager sponsor or a formal request for proposals. Breaking Defense says the current process takes days or weeks, and some projects have stalled for as long as 9 months." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And the caution?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Only Breaking Defense has reported this, and the Army has published no accuracy or false-pass figures for the AI assessment. The chief engineer on it told Breaking Defense the idea here is not to lessen the security requirements." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "On health, there's a study of what people in four countries actually ask a chatbot about their bodies." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Health, science & medicine",
      "headline": "Study of 15,555 real ChatGPT health conversations in four Global South countries rates about one in five high-risk",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "This is a preprint, not peer reviewed. Two authors worked from a consent-based data-donation corpus of 202,590 conversations from 1,252 users in India, Nigeria, Brazil and Pakistan, running from December 2022 to February 2026, and pulled out 15,555 health conversations." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And how many were unsafe?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Roughly one in five health conversations got a high-risk rating, with the risk concentrated in symptom and condition queries. The failures they name are unsafe medication or dosage guidance, harmful self-diagnosis, missing safety warnings, encouraging over-reliance on the model, and failing to refer people to professionals." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What's the part that surprised you?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Personalisation made it worse, not better. Supplying the user's own accumulated context raises the high-risk share by 13 to 17 percentage points, and about one in four conversations rated safe in isolation turns out to be unsafe for the specific person asking." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And the limits on it." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The corpus is 1,252 self-selected donors, not a representative sample. No institutional affiliation is printed on the paper. And it doesn't report which model versions produced the conversations." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "On policy and law, Anthropic is back in front of the appeals court over its fight with the Pentagon." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Policy, regulation & law",
      "headline": "Anthropic petitions the D.C. Circuit for rehearing of the Pentagon's supply-chain-risk designation",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Anthropic filed a petition on Tuesday asking the federal appeals court in Washington to rehear its appeal over the Pentagon's blacklisting of its models. Its lawyers wrote that the Secretary of War branded Anthropic a national-security supply chain risk because it declined to permit its models to be used for mass surveillance of Americans or lethal autonomous warfare, and publicly explained those AI-safety commitments." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Remind me where this stands." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "CNBC reports the Defense Department applied the label in March, which blocked defense contractors from using Anthropic's technology in their work for the agency. In September the appeals court upheld it, 2 to 1. But in a parallel case, a San Francisco federal judge ruled in August that the department had violated Anthropic's First Amendment rights and that the designation was illegal retaliation." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "So two courts, opposite answers." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "That's the ground Anthropic is asking the full appeals court to revisit. Its lawyers wrote that the panel ignored straightforward evidence the designation was retaliation for Anthropic's speech about AI safety. A petition for rehearing is a request, not a grant. Anthropic declined to comment beyond the filing, the Pentagon doesn't comment on pending litigation, and only CNBC has reported it." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Policy, regulation & law",
      "headline": "Eurobarometer: 42% of surveyed EU SMEs and mid-caps already use AI or algorithmic management, 49% distrust it for staff decisions",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "And there's new European survey data on who's actually using this at work." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The European Commission says 42% of the smaller and mid-sized European companies it consulted already use AI or algorithmic management, most of them in a limited and experimental way, with another 21% planning to. The uses are customer service at 47%, monitoring performance and productivity at 36%, and logistics and staff scheduling at 30%." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Do they like it?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "61% say using AI helps them take decisions, 75% think it will improve job quality, and 90% say people still take the decisions, with only 2% fully automated." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And yet there's a number that cuts against all of that." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "There is. 49% of these same companies say they do not trust artificial intelligence or automated systems for decisions related to workforce or staff matters. These are self-reported survey answers, not audited deployment data, and the Commission's own web page renders empty to automated reads, so we read the figures from its document service." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Which brings us to the machines and the money, and today they point in opposite directions." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Compute, chips & infrastructure",
      "headline": "ASML posts record €11,662 million Q3 net sales and says planned 2027 and 2028 supply will not meet customer demand",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "ASML reported third-quarter net sales of 11,662 million euros, against 9,326 million in the second quarter. Gross margin 57.5% against 54.0%, net income 4,960 million euros against 2,918 million. New lithography systems sold rose to 113 units from 86." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And the line that matters." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The chief executive says their supply as currently planned for both 2027 and 2028 will not fully meet the demand of their customers. They're looking at ways to raise 2028 capacity beyond the 30% increase already announced, and he says some customers are expressing an interest in placing orders for 2029 and even 2030." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Why does one company's guidance matter this much?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Because ASML is the chokepoint. If the machines that make the chips are short for two years, that's a physical ceiling on how fast AI compute gets built, whatever the capital says. Though note the demand statement is ASML's reading of customer forecasts, and that 2029 and 2030 interest is interest, not booked orders. ASML's own investor site refused automated reads, so we took the release from its securities filing." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Compute, chips & infrastructure",
      "headline": "Gartner: AI infrastructure needs $8.5 trillion of revenue against at most $3.6 trillion available, and 80% of neoclouds will not survive to 2030",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "And that's the supply side. The demand side is where Gartner comes in." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The Register reports Gartner expects AI providers to spend nearly $1 trillion on AI infrastructure in 2026, and a further $6 trillion from 2027 to 2030. At a 12% return on invested capital, Gartner estimates operators need $8.5 trillion in revenue to cover the capital spending and the financing costs." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Against what?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "No more than $3.6 trillion in enterprise and consumer AI revenue over that whole period. Gartner's report is titled to say capital write-downs could exceed $4 trillion by 2032, and it forecasts 80% of GPU-as-a-service providers won't survive as independent companies beyond 2030." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Is anyone safe in that picture?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The analyst told The Register, quote, not everyone is in trouble, and Gartner expects the hyperscalers to fare better because their businesses go beyond AI services. These are forecasts, not measured outcomes, reaching out to 2032. Only The Register has reported them, and Gartner's own report wasn't opened." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "And one last story, on what a consumer chatbot company turns out to be worth when the court is the one doing the selling." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Deployment & impact",
      "headline": "Character.AI agrees to sell assets to Disney for $20 million, with wind-down the stated alternative",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "This is an update on the bankruptcy we covered yesterday. TechCrunch reports Character.AI has agreed to sell some of its assets to Disney for $20 million, plus a non-exclusive perpetual license for its technology. In its court filing the company said it had assets and liabilities between $50 million and $100 million." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And if no buyer appears?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The chief executive said they hope the sale process will yield an actionable proposal, and that if such a bid is unavailable, the platform will be wound down. Closing is expected in late November or early December." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "So that's the valuation on the table." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Part of the assets, $20 million, in a court-supervised sale, with shutdown as the stated alternative. TechCrunch reports there isn't yet a bid or a buyer beyond that stalking-horse arrangement, the sale needs court approval, and it hasn't closed. Only TechCrunch carries these terms." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "outro",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "That's The AI Edge for today." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The full edition is on the site, with a link to every source behind every claim, so you can go and check the numbers yourself." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Our voices are AI-generated." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Listen in tomorrow for the next edition." }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

