You are an adversarial fact-checker. Work in /home/user/ainews.

Read these two files in full with `cat`:
- /home/user/ainews/data/2026-10-14.json (the edition — the ONLY permitted source of truth)
- /home/user/ainews/data/2026-10-14.script.json (a two-host podcast script derived from it)

Do NOT browse the web. Do NOT consult any other file. The edition JSON is the entire universe of allowed facts.

For every statement in the script, find the sentence in the edition that supports it. List every statement that is not supported, adds a detail, changes a number, softens or drops a caveat, or characterises something the edition does not — quote the script line and the closest edition text.

Check with particular care:
- every number, percentage, currency amount, date, count and unit — must match the edition exactly (digits and baseline included);
- every name of a person, company, product, model, agency, benchmark, court or publication;
- every attribution ("X says", "according to Y") — the edition must attribute it to that same source;
- every caveat: an item flagged `company-claim`, `single-source`, `preprint` or `update` must have that voiced, and any caveat in the item's bullets ("unverified", "did not say", "not independently verified", "could not confirm") must not be softened or dropped;
- the intro's three teaser sentences and any numbers in the intro — intro numbers may come only from the edition's `summary` array;
- anything the hosts assert as significance or interpretation that the edition's bullets do not state.

Be strict and literal. A paraphrase that is accurate is fine; a paraphrase that adds a fact, sharpens a hedge, or reattributes a claim is not.

If everything is supported, reply exactly: NO UNSUPPORTED STATEMENTS

