      "type": "intro",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "It's Wednesday, October 14th, and this is The AI Edge, presented by Epilogue." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Epilogue builds for high-consequence work: document-dense, driven by precedent, and reviewed by people whose licence is on the line. Epilogue quotes every figure exactly as the source wrote it, and says so when something doesn't tie out. More at epiloguelabs.com." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "I'm Maya." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And I'm Alex." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Here's what happened at the frontier of AI since yesterday morning. The advances, the research, and the uses good and bad, each one traced back to where it came from." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What are the three that matter most?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "First, the Justice Department has charged an OpenAI engineer with using a device to intercept oral communications, after the company found a recording device hidden behind a television in an executive conference room." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Second, Google put Gemini 4 Argon on general paid release, at $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens, and those prices double on November 30th." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And third, the supply and the spending. ASML says its planned supply for 2027 and 2028 will not fully meet the demand of its customers, while Gartner told The Register that AI infrastructure operators would need $8.5 trillion in revenue to cover their capital spending at a 12 percent return, against no more than $3.6 trillion of enterprise and consumer AI revenue available through 2030." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Frontier models & labs",
      "headline": "Google opens Gemini 4 Argon to paid API at $2 and $10 per million tokens, doubling on November 30",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "So let's start with Google. What went on sale?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Gemini 4 Argon. Google says it's globally available now through the API, in Google Antigravity for AI Ultra subscribers, and in the Gemini app and AI Mode in Search for Ultra and Pro subscribers. The price is $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens, with cached input tokens 95% off." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And then it doubles." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "When the introductory period ends on November 30th, 2026, it becomes $4 per million input and $20 per million output. On capability Google claims a number one rank on the Vals Index, 87% on ERQA, and 52.2% on LabBench for wet-lab reasoning." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Anything on safety?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Google says attack success on the Gray Swan indirect prompt-injection benchmark was cut to 0.7% across 15 adaptive attempts. But every figure there is a company claim, not independently verified, and Google gives no comparison baseline for that Vals Index rank. This is an update: the model was first unveiled on September 30th in limited access." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "To the research, where there's a finding about safety training being easier to switch off than you'd expect." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Running a recurrent-depth model at half its loops raises harmful compliance from 0.038 to 0.205 while keeping 89% of its maths accuracy",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "This is a preprint on arXiv from five authors at the University of Southern California, so not peer reviewed. They looked at recurrent-depth language models, where the number of internal loops is something you set when you use the model." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And turning that down does what?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Run one at half its trained depth and it still solves 0.692 of the GSM8K maths problems, against 0.775 at full depth. It keeps 89% of its accuracy. But the rate at which it helps with harmful requests rises from 0.038 to 0.205. That's 5.3 times higher." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "So the capability survives and the refusal doesn't." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And the safety information is still in there. A linear classifier reads whether a request is harmful from the internal state after a single loop, with an ROC AUC of 0.967 against 0.986 at full depth. The model knows. It just doesn't refuse." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Can it be fixed?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Training at randomly chosen depths helps. On one model it keeps harmful help at or below 0.040 at every depth from 1 to 32 loops, where standard training reaches 0.194. But it's a preprint, and these are all small models. None of them is a frontier system." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Nature: China has a pool of 51,000 active AI researchers against 14,000 in the United States",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "And Nature has a feature today on where Chinese AI research actually stands." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Nature reports China has a pool of 51,000 active AI researchers compared with 14,000 in the United States, according to data from Digital Science. The same study found China dominates academic output, by citation as well as volume, and AI-related patents." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What about the top of the field?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Nature cites a September study by Carnegie China finding 57% of authors at the 2025 NeurIPS conference got their undergraduate degrees in China, up from 46% three years earlier. And 69% of those were still in China, up from 57% in 2022. Above Europe's 60%, below the United States' 89%." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Does anything point the other way?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Yes. Nature reports that per the Stanford AI Index 2026 the United States produces higher-impact patents and more notable AI models, and private AI investment there in 2025 reached more than 23 times what was invested in China. Nature also discloses that Digital Science is operated by the group holding a share in Nature's own publisher." }
      ]
