        {
          "headline": "Anthropic petitions the D.C. Circuit for rehearing of the Pentagon's supply-chain-risk designation",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "CNBC", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/13/anthropic-wants-court-in-dc-to-rehear-appeal-of-pentagon-blacklist-.html" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "Anthropic filed a petition on Tuesday asking the federal appeals court in Washington, D.C. to rehear its appeal over the Pentagon's blacklisting of its models. Its attorneys wrote: \"The Secretary of War branded Anthropic — an American AI company whose models help defend the nation— a national-security 'supply chain risk' because it declined to permit its models to be used for mass surveillance of Americans or lethal autonomous warfare and publicly explained those AI-safety commitments. A divided panel upheld that extraordinary designation.\" They also wrote that the court ignored \"straightforward evidence the designation was retaliation for Anthropic's speech about AI safety\".",
            "Per CNBC, the Defense Department applied the supply-chain-risk label in March, blocking defense contractors from using Anthropic's technology in their work with the agency; in September the D.C. Circuit upheld it 2-1, rejecting Anthropic's argument that the ban was arbitrary, unauthorized and unconstitutional. In a parallel case, a San Francisco federal judge ruled in August that the DOD had violated Anthropic's First Amendment rights and that the designation amounted to illegal retaliation.",
            "The two rulings point opposite ways on the same facts, which is the ground Anthropic is asking the full D.C. Circuit to revisit.",
            "CNBC notes Anthropic signed a $200 million contract with the Pentagon in July 2025, and that talks collapsed in September of that year over Claude's deployment on the DOD's GenAI.mil platform. A petition for rehearing is a request, not a grant. Anthropic declined to comment beyond the filing and a Pentagon spokesperson said the agency does not comment on pending litigation. Only CNBC has reported the filing."
          ],
          "topics": ["anthropic", "pentagon", "us-federal-policy", "military"],
          "storylines": ["anthropic-pentagon-split"],
          "impact": "neutral",
          "flags": ["single-source"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Judge grants Apple expedited access to OpenAI interview records and three depositions in its trade-secrets suit",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "TechCrunch", "url": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/13/judge-orders-openai-to-turn-over-hiring-records-in-apple-trade-secrets-case/" },
            { "name": "9to5Mac", "url": "https://9to5mac.com/2026/10/13/apple-wins-early-access-to-openai-documents-and-employee-testimony-in-trade-secrets-lawsuit/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "U.S. District Judge Edward Davila ruled Apple may view documents from OpenAI's interviews with former Apple employees who applied to its consumer hardware and robotics groups, including interview checklists, guides, instructions, manuals, scripts, technical presentations and records referencing Apple technology or components. The court gave OpenAI five days to turn over materials already collected and 14 days for other materials related to the request.",
            "Davila also allowed access to private LINE messages between named individuals including OpenAI employees Chang Liu and Alyssa Peng, and approved depositions of Liu, Peng and OpenAI chief hardware officer Tang Yew Tan — all three former Apple employees.",
            "Expedited discovery before the usual schedule is an unusual order, and it reaches into how one frontier lab interviews a rival's staff.",
            "The judge denied expedited access to OpenAI R&D in areas such as metal finishes and power management, and denied broader discovery requests including OpenAI's hardware research, while leaving the door open for narrowed requests. Apple filed the suit in July. This is a discovery ruling, not a finding on the merits."
          ],
          "topics": ["openai", "apple", "us-federal-policy", "copyright"],
          "impact": "neutral",
          "flags": []
