  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "intro",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "It's Wednesday, October 14th, and this is The AI Edge, presented by Epilogue."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Epilogue builds for high-consequence work: document-dense, driven by precedent, and reviewed by people whose licence is on the line. Epilogue quotes every figure exactly as the source wrote it, and says so when something doesn't tie out. More at epiloguelabs.com."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "I'm Maya."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And I'm Alex."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Here's what happened at the frontier of AI since yesterday morning. The advances, the research, and the uses good and bad, each one traced back to where it came from."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "What are the three that matter most?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "First, the Justice Department has charged an OpenAI engineer with using a device to intercept oral communications, after the company found a recording device hidden behind a television in an executive conference room."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Second, Google put Gemini 4 Argon on general paid release, at $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens, and those prices double on November 30th."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "And third, the supply and the spending. ASML says its planned supply for 2027 and 2028 will not fully meet the demand of its customers, while Gartner told The Register that AI infrastructure operators would need $8.5 trillion in revenue to cover their capital spending at a 12 percent return, against no more than $3.6 trillion of enterprise and consumer AI revenue available through 2030."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Frontier models & labs",
      "headline": "Google opens Gemini 4 Argon to paid API at $2 and $10 per million tokens, doubling on November 30",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Let's start with Google. What went on sale?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Gemini 4 Argon. Google says it's globally available now through the API, in Google Antigravity for AI Ultra subscribers, and in the Gemini app and AI Mode in Search for Ultra and Pro subscribers. The price is $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens, with cached input tokens 95% off."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And then it doubles."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "When the introductory period ends on November 30th, 2026, it becomes $4 per million input and $20 per million output. On capability Google claims a number one rank on the Vals Index, 87% on ERQA, and 52.2% on LabBench for wet-lab reasoning."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Anything on safety?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Google says attack success on the Gray Swan indirect prompt-injection benchmark was cut to 0.7% across 15 adaptive attempts. But every figure there is a company claim, not independently verified, and Google gives no comparison baseline for that Vals Index rank. This is an update: the model was first unveiled on September 30th in limited access."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "To the research, and safety training that is easier to switch off than you'd expect."
