    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Running a recurrent-depth model at half its loops raises harmful compliance from 0.038 to 0.205 while keeping 89% of its maths accuracy",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "This is a preprint on arXiv from five authors at the University of Southern California, so not peer reviewed. They looked at recurrent-depth language models, where the number of internal loops is something you set when you use the model."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And turning that down does what?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Run one at half its trained depth and it still solves 0.692 of the GSM8K maths problems, against 0.775 at full depth. It keeps 89% of its accuracy. But the rate at which it helps with harmful requests rises from 0.038 to 0.205. That's 5.3 times higher."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "So the capability survives and the refusal doesn't."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "And the safety information is still in there. A linear classifier reads whether a request is harmful from the internal state after a single loop, with an ROC AUC of 0.967 against 0.986 at full depth."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Can it be fixed?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Training at randomly chosen depths helps. On one model it keeps harmful help at or below 0.040 at every depth from 1 to 32 loops, where standard training reaches 0.194. But it's a preprint, and these are all small models. None of them is a frontier system."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Nature: China has a pool of 51,000 active AI researchers against 14,000 in the United States",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And Nature has a feature today on where Chinese AI research actually stands."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Nature reports China has a pool of 51,000 active AI researchers compared with 14,000 in the United States, according to data from Digital Science. The same study found China dominates academic output, by citation as well as volume, and AI-related patents."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "What about the top of the field?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Nature cites a September study by Carnegie China finding 57% of authors at the 2025 NeurIPS conference got their undergraduate degrees in China, up from 46% three years earlier. And 69% of those were still in China, up from 57% in 2022. Above Europe's 60%, below the United States' 89%."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Does anything point the other way?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Yes. Nature reports that per the Stanford AI Index 2026 the United States produces higher-impact patents and more notable AI models, and private AI investment there in 2025 reached more than 23 times what was invested in China. Nature also discloses that Digital Science is operated by the group holding a share in Nature's own publisher, and notes publication data can skew the picture, because Chinese firms are more likely to publish papers than US start-ups."
        }
