{
  "type": "item",
  "section": "Health, science & medicine",
  "headline": "Study of 15,555 real ChatGPT health conversations in four Global South countries rates about one in five high-risk",
  "lines": [
    {
      "host": "A",
      "text": "A preprint on arXiv, not peer reviewed, and a single source. Two authors worked from a consent-based data-donation corpus of 202,590 conversations from 1,252 users in India, Nigeria, Brazil and Pakistan, running from December 2022 to February 2026, and pulled out 15,555 health conversations."
    },
    {
      "host": "B",
      "text": "How many were unsafe?"
    },
    {
      "host": "A",
      "text": "Roughly one in five got a high-risk rating, concentrated in symptom and condition queries. The failures they name are unsafe medication or dosage guidance, harmful self-diagnosis, missing safety warnings, encouraging over-reliance on the model, and failing to refer people to professionals."
    },
    {
      "host": "B",
      "text": "What's the part that stands out?"
    },
    {
      "host": "A",
      "text": "Personalisation made it worse, not better. Supplying the user's own accumulated context raises the high-risk share by 13 to 17 percentage points, and about one in four conversations rated safe in isolation turns out to be unsafe for the specific person asking."
    },
    {
      "host": "B",
      "text": "And the limits."
    },
    {
      "host": "A",
      "text": "The corpus is 1,252 self-selected donors, not a representative sample. No institutional affiliation is printed on the paper, and it doesn't report which model versions produced the conversations."
    }
  ]
},
{
  "type": "transition",
  "lines": [
    {
      "host": "B",
      "text": "On policy and law, Anthropic is back before the appeals court over the Pentagon."
    }
