{
  "type": "item",
  "section": "Policy, regulation & law",
  "headline": "Anthropic petitions the D.C. Circuit for rehearing of the Pentagon's supply-chain-risk designation",
  "lines": [
    {
      "host": "A",

          "host": "A",
          "text": "Anthropic filed a petition on Tuesday asking the federal appeals court in Washington to rehear its appeal over the Pentagon's blacklisting of its models. Its lawyers wrote that the Secretary of War branded Anthropic a national-security supply chain risk because it declined to permit its models to be used for mass surveillance of Americans or lethal autonomous warfare."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Remind me where this stands."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "CNBC reports the Defense Department applied the label in March, which blocked defense contractors from using Anthropic's technology in their work for the agency. In September the appeals court upheld it, 2 to 1. But in a parallel case, a San Francisco federal judge ruled in August that the department had violated Anthropic's First Amendment rights and that the designation amounted to illegal retaliation."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "So two courts, opposite answers."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "That's the ground Anthropic wants the full appeals court to revisit. Its lawyers wrote that the court ignored straightforward evidence the designation was retaliation for Anthropic's speech about AI safety. A petition for rehearing is a request, not a grant. Anthropic declined to comment beyond the filing, the Pentagon doesn't comment on pending litigation, and CNBC is a single source on it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Which brings us to the machines and the money. Two reports today, one on each."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Compute, chips & infrastructure",
      "headline": "ASML posts record €11,662 million Q3 net sales and says planned 2027 and 2028 supply will not meet customer demand",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "ASML reported third-quarter net sales of 11,662 million euros, against 9,326 million in the second quarter. Gross margin 57.5% against 54.0%, net income 4,960 million euros against 2,918 million. New lithography systems sold rose to 113 units from 86."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And the line that matters."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "The chief executive says their supply as currently planned for both 2027 and 2028 will not fully meet the demand of their customers. They're looking at ways to raise 2028 capacity beyond the 30% increase already announced, and he says some customers are expressing an interest in placing orders for 2029 and even 2030."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Why does one company's guidance matter this much?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Because ASML is the chokepoint. If the machines that make the chips are short for two years, that's a physical ceiling on how fast AI compute gets built, whatever the capital says. Though the demand statement is ASML's reading of customer forecasts, and that 2029 and 2030 interest is interest, not booked orders."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Compute, chips & infrastructure",
      "headline": "Gartner: AI infrastructure needs $8.5 trillion of revenue against at most $3.6 trillion available, and 80% of neoclouds will not survive to 2030",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "That's the supply side. The demand side is where Gartner comes in."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "The Register reports Gartner expects AI providers to spend nearly $1 trillion on AI infrastructure in 2026, and a further $6 trillion from 2027 to 2030. At a 12 percent return on invested capital, Gartner estimates operators need $8.5 trillion in revenue to cover the capital spending and the financing costs."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Against what?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "No more than $3.6 trillion in enterprise and consumer AI revenue over that whole period. Gartner's report is titled to say capital write-downs could exceed $4 trillion by 2032, and it forecasts 80 percent of GPU-as-a-service providers won't survive as independent companies beyond 2030."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Is anyone safe in that picture?"
